window.REV_FLIP=window.REV_FLIP||{};window.REV_FLIP["django"]=[{"id":"django-f0001","card":"q-django-001-1-django-basics-project-and-mvt","sec":"django-s002","q":"What problem does Django solve, and what do you get out of the box compared with a micro-framework like Flask?","a":"<p>Django is a batteries-included Python web framework. It ships an ORM, migrations, an admin site, authentication, forms, templating, CSRF and clickjacking protection, and a dev server. Flask gives you routing and little else, so you pick and wire each piece yourself. Trade-off: Django is faster to start for data-driven sites but more opinionated and heavier.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0002","card":"q-django-001-1-django-basics-project-and-mvt","sec":"django-s002","q":"What is the difference between a Django project and a Django app?","a":"<p>A project is the whole site: settings, root URL config, and the WSGI/ASGI entry points. An app is a self-contained feature package (models, views, templates, URLs) that is plugged into the project through <code>INSTALLED_APPS</code>. One project holds many apps, and a good app can be reused in another project.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0003","card":"q-django-001-1-django-basics-project-and-mvt","sec":"django-s002","q":"What does <code>django-admin startproject mysite .</code> create, and why is the trailing dot used?","a":"<p>It creates <code>manage.py</code> and a config package named <code>mysite/</code> containing <code>settings.py</code>, <code>urls.py</code>, <code>wsgi.py</code> and <code>asgi.py</code>. The trailing <code>.</code> creates the project in the current folder instead of adding an extra nested folder with the same name.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0004","card":"q-django-001-1-django-basics-project-and-mvt","sec":"django-s002","q":"What is the role of <code>manage.py</code> compared with <code>django-admin</code>?","a":"<p>Both run Django management commands. <code>manage.py</code> is a thin wrapper that also sets <code>DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE</code> for the current project, so commands like <code>runserver</code>, <code>migrate</code> and <code>shell</code> use that project's settings. <code>django-admin</code> is global and needs the settings module set manually, so it is mainly used for <code>startproject</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0005","card":"q-django-001-1-django-basics-project-and-mvt","sec":"django-s002","q":"What does <code>DEBUG = True</code> change in Django, and why must it be <code>False</code> in production?","a":"<p>With <code>DEBUG = True</code>, errors show a detailed page with the traceback, settings and local variables, and a 404 lists the URL patterns. In production that leaks internals and secrets. With <code>DEBUG = False</code> you must set <code>ALLOWED_HOSTS</code>, and Django shows plain error pages. Static files are also no longer served by <code>runserver</code> automatically.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0006","card":"q-django-001-1-django-basics-project-and-mvt","sec":"django-s002","q":"In Django's MVT pattern, which layer plays the role of the controller in classic MVC?","a":"<p>The View. A Django view receives the request and returns a response, so it is closer to an MVC controller. The Template is the presentation layer that MVC calls the view. The Model holds the data and its rules. Django's URL dispatcher also takes part in the controller role by routing the request to a view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0007","card":"q-django-001-1-django-basics-project-and-mvt","sec":"django-s002","q":"Walk through what happens from the browser requesting a URL to Django returning a page.","a":"<p>The request reaches Django through middleware. The URL resolver matches the path against <code>urlpatterns</code> and picks a view. The view may query models and renders a template with a context, then returns an <code>HttpResponse</code>. The response passes back through middleware to the browser.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0008","card":"q-django-002-apps-and-mvt","sec":"django-s003","q":"After <code>python manage.py startapp blog</code>, the app's views are not picked up and its models are not migrated. What step is probably missing?","a":"<p>The app is not listed in <code>INSTALLED_APPS</code> in <code>settings.py</code>. Django only discovers models, migrations, templates under <code>app/templates</code> and management commands for registered apps. Add <code>'blog'</code> or its <code>AppConfig</code> path <code>'blog.apps.BlogConfig'</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0009","card":"q-django-002-apps-and-mvt","sec":"django-s003","q":"Why split a Django project into several apps instead of one big app?","a":"<p>Clear boundaries make code easier to navigate, test and reuse, and separate teams can own separate apps. Each app can own its models, URLs and templates. The cost is cross-app coupling to manage, so split by business capability (accounts, billing, notes) and not by technical layer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0010","card":"q-django-002-apps-and-mvt","sec":"django-s003","q":"What does this minimal Django view do, and what must be true for it to run?","a":"<p>It returns a plain-text/HTML response with the body <code>Hello, world!</code> for any request routed to it. A view always takes the <code>request</code> and must return an <code>HttpResponse</code> (or subclass) or raise an exception. It only runs once a <code>path()</code> in a URLconf points to it.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from django.http import HttpResponse\n\ndef home(request):\n    return HttpResponse(&#x27;Hello, world!&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"django-f0011","card":"q-django-003-templates-and-dtl","sec":"django-s004","q":"Why does Django convention put templates in <code>app/templates/app/file.html</code> instead of <code>app/templates/file.html</code>?","a":"<p>Django merges the <code>templates</code> folders of all installed apps into one search namespace. Two apps with a <code>index.html</code> would collide, and the first match wins. The inner folder named after the app namespaces the template, so <code>render(request, 'blog/index.html', ...)</code> is unambiguous.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0012","card":"q-django-003-templates-and-dtl","sec":"django-s004","q":"What are the arguments of <code>render()</code> in Django, and what does it return?","a":"<p><code>render(request, template_name, context=None)</code> loads the template, renders it with the context dict, and returns an <code>HttpResponse</code>. The <code>request</code> is needed so context processors (such as <code>user</code> and <code>csrf_token</code>) can add their data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0013","card":"q-django-003-templates-and-dtl","sec":"django-s004","q":"In the Django template language, what is the difference between <code>{{ ... }}</code> and <code>{% ... %}</code>?","a":"<p><code>{{ ... }}</code> outputs a value, with variable lookup and filters, e.g. <code>{{ title|upper }}</code>. <code>{% ... %}</code> is a tag that runs logic such as <code>for</code>, <code>if</code>, <code>url</code>, <code>extends</code> or <code>include</code>. Output from <code>{{ }}</code> is HTML-escaped by default, which protects against XSS.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0014","card":"q-django-003-templates-and-dtl","sec":"django-s004","q":"Django templates auto-escape variables. When does that matter, and how would you intentionally turn it off?","a":"<p>Auto-escaping turns characters like <code>&lt;</code> into <code>&amp;lt;</code>, so user input cannot inject script (XSS). To output trusted HTML use the <code>|safe</code> filter or <code>{% autoescape off %}</code>. Use it only on content you control or have sanitised, because it removes the protection.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0015","card":"q-django-004-self-contained-apps-include","sec":"django-s005","q":"In Django, why would you write <code>path('', include('blog.urls'))</code> in the project URLs instead of importing the views from the app?","a":"<p><code>include()</code> takes the app's URLconf by dotted string, so the project does not import app code directly. The app owns its own routes, and removing or swapping the app only requires changing one line. It also lets you mount the app under a prefix like <code>path('blog/', include('blog.urls'))</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0016","card":"q-django-004-self-contained-apps-include","sec":"django-s005","q":"What does <code>path('smart/', include('notes.urls'))</code> do to a route <code>path('notes', ...)</code> defined in the app's <code>urls.py</code>?","a":"<p>Django strips the matched prefix and passes the rest to the included URLconf. The route becomes reachable at <code>smart/notes</code>. The prefix is set once at the include and applies to all app routes.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0017","card":"q-django-005-2-admin-and-built-in-auth","sec":"django-s006","q":"<code>runserver</code> prints a warning about unapplied migrations right after creating a new project. What does it mean and what do you run?","a":"<p>The built-in apps (admin, auth, sessions, contenttypes) have migrations that have not been applied, so their tables do not exist. Run <code>python manage.py migrate</code>. Without it the admin login fails because the user and session tables are missing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0018","card":"q-django-005-2-admin-and-built-in-auth","sec":"django-s006","q":"How do you create the first admin user in Django, and why should you not weaken its password rules?","a":"<p>Run <code>python manage.py createsuperuser</code>. Django applies <code>AUTH_PASSWORD_VALIDATORS</code>, which reject common, short or numeric-only passwords. Bypassing them in production creates an easily guessed account with full access to all data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0019","card":"q-django-005-2-admin-and-built-in-auth","sec":"django-s006","q":"How does Django store user passwords, and can you recover a forgotten one?","a":"<p>It stores a salted hash, using PBKDF2 by default, with the algorithm and iterations encoded in the stored string. The original password cannot be recovered. You can only reset it by setting a new one, for example with <code>changepassword</code> or <code>set_password()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0020","card":"q-django-005-2-admin-and-built-in-auth","sec":"django-s006","q":"When is the Django admin a good fit, and when is it not?","a":"<p>It is good for internal staff to manage data: list, search, filter, create, edit and delete, with an action history. It is not meant as the public end-user interface. It is built around model CRUD, so custom workflows and polished UX should be done in your own views.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0021","card":"q-django-006-protecting-a-view","sec":"django-s007","q":"How do you restrict a function-based Django view to logged-in users, and what happens to anonymous users?","a":"<p><code>@login_required</code> checks <code>request.user.is_authenticated</code>. Anonymous users are redirected to <code>settings.LOGIN_URL</code> with a <code>?next=</code> parameter holding the original path. The view body runs only for authenticated users.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required\n\n@login_required\ndef dashboard(request):\n    ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"django-f0022","card":"q-django-006-protecting-a-view","sec":"django-s007","q":"What does the <code>login_url</code> argument of <code>@login_required(login_url='/login')</code> change?","a":"<p>It overrides <code>settings.LOGIN_URL</code> for that view, so anonymous users are redirected to that URL instead. Without either setting, Django redirects to the default <code>/accounts/login/</code>, which returns 404 if you have not defined it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0023","card":"q-django-006-protecting-a-view","sec":"django-s007","q":"Where does <code>@login_required</code> send the user after they log in successfully?","a":"<p>Django adds <code>?next=&lt;original path&gt;</code> to the login redirect. The built-in <code>LoginView</code> reads <code>next</code> and redirects there after a successful login. If <code>next</code> is missing it uses <code>LOGIN_REDIRECT_URL</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0024","card":"q-django-007-3-models-migrations-and-orm","sec":"django-s008","q":"What is an ORM and how does Django's ORM map code to the database?","a":"<p>An ORM maps database tables to classes and rows to objects. In Django each <code>models.Model</code> subclass is a table and each field attribute is a column. You query with Python (<code>Model.objects.filter(...)</code>) and Django builds the SQL. Benefits: portability across databases and less hand-written SQL. Cost: complex queries may need raw SQL, and careless use causes N+1 queries.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0025","card":"q-django-007-3-models-migrations-and-orm","sec":"django-s008","q":"What is the correct order of commands after changing a Django model, and why are there two steps?","a":"<p>Run <code>makemigrations</code>, then <code>migrate</code>. <code>makemigrations</code> compares models with migration history and writes a migration file, which is a versioned description of the schema change that you can review and commit. <code>migrate</code> applies unapplied migrations to the database and records them in <code>django_migrations</code>. Splitting them lets you review, version-control and run the same change on every environment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0026","card":"q-django-007-3-models-migrations-and-orm","sec":"django-s008","q":"What happens to the database when you run <code>makemigrations</code> but not <code>migrate</code>?","a":"<p>Nothing. <code>makemigrations</code> only writes a file in the app's <code>migrations/</code> folder. The schema changes only when <code>migrate</code> runs, so code using the new column fails with an error such as \"no such column\".</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0027","card":"q-django-007-3-models-migrations-and-orm","sec":"django-s008","q":"Why is <code>max_length</code> required on a <code>CharField</code>, and when would you use <code>TextField</code> instead?","a":"<p><code>CharField</code> maps to a <code>VARCHAR(n)</code> column on most databases, so it needs a length, and it is also used for form validation. <code>TextField</code> has no required length and fits long free text such as a body. Use <code>CharField</code> for bounded values like titles or names.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0028","card":"q-django-007-3-models-migrations-and-orm","sec":"django-s008","q":"What does <code>auto_now_add=True</code> do on a <code>DateTimeField</code>, and how does it differ from <code>auto_now=True</code>?","a":"<p><code>auto_now_add=True</code> sets the value once, when the row is first created. <code>auto_now=True</code> updates it on every <code>save()</code>, which suits a \"last modified\" field. Both make the field non-editable, so it does not appear in model forms or the admin edit form.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0029","card":"q-django-007-3-models-migrations-and-orm","sec":"django-s008","q":"What is a migration file made of, and what are its dependencies?","a":"<p>It is a Python class with a <code>dependencies</code> list and an <code>operations</code> list, such as <code>CreateModel</code> or <code>AddField</code>. Dependencies name the earlier migration it builds on, so Django applies them in order. Migration files should be committed to version control and not edited after they are applied elsewhere.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0030","card":"q-django-008-registering-a-model-in-admin","sec":"django-s009","q":"A new model does not appear in the Django admin site. What is missing?","a":"<p>Models must be registered in the app's <code>admin.py</code>, for example <code>admin.site.register(MyModel)</code> or the <code>@admin.register(MyModel)</code> decorator. Registration is explicit, so only models you choose are exposed to staff.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0031","card":"q-django-008-registering-a-model-in-admin","sec":"django-s009","q":"In a <code>ModelAdmin</code>, what does <code>list_display</code> do, and what do you see without it?","a":"<p><code>list_display</code> chooses which columns appear on the admin changelist page. Without it the list shows one column using the model's <code>__str__</code>, which defaults to something like <code>Article object (1)</code>. You can also add <code>list_filter</code>, <code>search_fields</code> and <code>readonly_fields</code> to the same class.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from django.contrib import admin\n\nclass ArticleAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin):\n    list_display = (&#x27;title&#x27;,)\n\nadmin.site.register(Article, ArticleAdmin)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"django-f0032","card":"q-django-008-registering-a-model-in-admin","sec":"django-s009","q":"What is the difference between <code>admin.site.register(Model)</code> and <code>admin.site.register(Model, ModelAdminClass)</code>?","a":"<p>The first uses default admin behaviour. The second applies your <code>ModelAdmin</code> customisations such as columns, filters, search and read-only fields. Both put the model in the admin.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0033","card":"q-django-009-django-shell-and-querysets","sec":"django-s010","q":"What is a QuerySet in Django and why is it described as lazy?","a":"<p>A QuerySet represents a database query as a collection of objects. It does not hit the database when created or chained with <code>filter()</code> or <code>exclude()</code>. The SQL runs when it is evaluated, for example by iterating, slicing with a step, <code>list()</code>, <code>len()</code> or <code>bool()</code>. Results are cached after the first evaluation. Lazy chaining lets you build a query in steps and run it once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0034","card":"q-django-009-django-shell-and-querysets","sec":"django-s010","q":"What is the difference between <code>Model.objects.get(pk=1)</code> and <code>Model.objects.filter(pk=1)</code>?","a":"<p><code>get()</code> returns exactly one object. It raises <code>DoesNotExist</code> if there is none and <code>MultipleObjectsReturned</code> if there are several. <code>filter()</code> returns a QuerySet that can be empty or hold many rows and never raises for no match. Use <code>get()</code> when you expect one row and <code>filter()</code> for sets.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0035","card":"q-django-009-django-shell-and-querysets","sec":"django-s010","q":"What do <code>filter(title__startswith='My')</code> and <code>filter(text__icontains='django')</code> do, and what is the <code>__</code> syntax?","a":"<p>The double underscore separates a field name from a lookup (or follows relations). <code>startswith</code> matches a prefix (case-sensitive in principle, though SQLite compares ASCII case-insensitively). <code>icontains</code> is a case-insensitive \"contains\". Other lookups include <code>exact</code>, <code>gt</code>, <code>gte</code>, <code>lt</code>, <code>in</code> and <code>isnull</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0036","card":"q-django-009-django-shell-and-querysets","sec":"django-s010","q":"What is the difference between <code>filter()</code> and <code>exclude()</code>, and what does chaining them do?","a":"<p><code>filter()</code> keeps matching rows and <code>exclude()</code> removes matching rows. Chaining combines them with AND, so <code>qs.filter(text__icontains='x').exclude(title__icontains='x')</code> keeps rows whose text contains x and whose title does not.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0037","card":"q-django-009-django-shell-and-querysets","sec":"django-s010","q":"What does <code>Model.objects.create(title='A', text='B')</code> do compared with creating an instance and calling <code>save()</code>?","a":"<p><code>create()</code> builds the object and saves it in one call, returning the saved instance. <code>Model(title='A', text='B').save()</code> is equivalent but gives you a chance to change the object before saving. Both run one <code>INSERT</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0038","card":"q-django-009-django-shell-and-querysets","sec":"django-s010","q":"How would you filter rows whose id is 1, 2 or 3 in the Django ORM?","a":"<p>Use the <code>__in</code> lookup, <code>Model.objects.filter(id__in=[1, 2, 3])</code>. It becomes a SQL <code>IN (...)</code> clause. To express OR across different fields use <code>Q</code> objects: <code>filter(Q(a=1) | Q(b=2))</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0039","card":"q-django-009-django-shell-and-querysets","sec":"django-s010","q":"Why use <code>python manage.py shell</code> instead of a plain <code>python</code> session to try ORM queries?","a":"<p><code>manage.py shell</code> sets <code>DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE</code> and calls <code>django.setup()</code>, so models, the database connection and installed apps are ready. A plain interpreter would raise <code>ImproperlyConfigured</code> or <code>AppRegistryNotReady</code> when importing models.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0040","card":"q-django-010-adding-a-field-to-an-existing-model-like","sec":"django-s011","q":"Which Django field type would you choose for a likes counter, and why not a plain <code>IntegerField</code>?","a":"<p><code>PositiveIntegerField</code> or <code>PositiveSmallIntegerField</code>, depending on expected size. A like count cannot be negative, and the positive variants add a database check constraint and validation. <code>PositiveSmallIntegerField</code> holds 0 to 32767 and uses less storage. Use <code>PositiveIntegerField</code> or <code>BigIntegerField</code> if the count may exceed that.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0041","card":"q-django-010-adding-a-field-to-an-existing-model-like","sec":"django-s011","q":"You add <code>likes = models.PositiveSmallIntegerField()</code> with no default to a model that already has rows. What happens on <code>makemigrations</code>?","a":"<p>Django cannot fill the new non-null column for existing rows, so it asks you to provide a one-off default or to cancel and set a default in the model. Setting <code>default=0</code> on the field avoids the prompt and gives old rows a value.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0042","card":"q-django-010-adding-a-field-to-an-existing-model-like","sec":"django-s011","q":"What does the new migration for an added field look like, and how does it relate to the first one?","a":"<p>It is a numbered file like <code>0002_&lt;name&gt;.py</code> with an <code>AddField</code> operation and a dependency on <code>0001_initial</code>. Migrations form a chain, and <code>migrate</code> applies the missing ones in dependency order.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0043","card":"q-django-010-adding-a-field-to-an-existing-model-like","sec":"django-s011","q":"What is a race condition when incrementing a likes counter with <code>note.likes += 1; note.save()</code>, and how do you avoid it?","a":"<p>Two requests can read the same value and each write value+1, so one like is lost. Use an <code>F</code> expression, <code>Model.objects.filter(pk=pk).update(likes=F('likes') + 1)</code>, so the database does the increment atomically in one statement.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0044","card":"q-django-011-4-dynamic-pages-list-and-detail","sec":"django-s012","q":"How do you display all rows of a model on a page using a function-based Django view?","a":"<p>Query in the view and pass the result in the context: <code>articles = Article.objects.all()</code> then <code>return render(request, 'blog/article_list.html', {'articles': articles})</code>. In the template loop with <code>{% for a in articles %}{{ a.title }}{% endfor %}</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from django.shortcuts import render\nfrom .models import Article\n\ndef article_list(request):\n    ...</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"django-f0045","card":"q-django-011-4-dynamic-pages-list-and-detail","sec":"django-s012","q":"Why should you not name a view function <code>list</code> in Django?","a":"<p>It shadows Python's built-in <code>list</code> in that module, so later calls to <code>list(...)</code> break in confusing ways. Prefer names like <code>article_list</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0046","card":"q-django-011-4-dynamic-pages-list-and-detail","sec":"django-s012","q":"In a Django template, how do you loop over a list and show a message when it is empty?","a":"<p>Use <code>{% for x in items %}...{% empty %}No items{% endfor %}</code>. The <code>empty</code> branch runs when the iterable is empty or missing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0047","card":"q-django-011-4-dynamic-pages-list-and-detail","sec":"django-s012","q":"Why is <code>{% for %}</code> closed with <code>{% endfor %}</code> and not with indentation or braces?","a":"<p>The Django template language is not Python. Tags that open a block need an explicit end tag, such as <code>endfor</code>, <code>endif</code> or <code>endblock</code>. Whitespace has no meaning.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0048","card":"q-django-012-detail-view-with-a-path-parameter","sec":"django-s013","q":"What does <code>path('articles/&lt;int:pk&gt;', views.detail)</code> do, and what happens to a request for <code>articles/abc</code>?","a":"<p><code>&lt;int:pk&gt;</code> is a path converter. It matches digits only, converts the value to <code>int</code>, and passes it to the view as the keyword argument <code>pk</code>. <code>articles/abc</code> does not match, so Django continues to other patterns and finally returns 404. Other converters are <code>str</code>, <code>slug</code>, <code>uuid</code> and <code>path</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0049","card":"q-django-012-detail-view-with-a-path-parameter","sec":"django-s013","q":"In a function-based detail view, what status does the user get if <code>Model.objects.get(pk=pk)</code> finds nothing and you do not catch the exception, and how do you return a proper 404?","a":"<p>An uncaught <code>DoesNotExist</code> becomes a 500 error. Catch it and <code>raise Http404(...)</code>, or simply use <code>get_object_or_404(Model, pk=pk)</code>, which does exactly that. A 404 correctly tells the client the resource does not exist.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0050","card":"q-django-012-detail-view-with-a-path-parameter","sec":"django-s013","q":"Why is returning 404 better than 500 for a missing record?","a":"<p>404 is a client error meaning \"not found\", so crawlers, caches and monitoring treat it correctly. A 500 signals a server bug, triggers alerts and can leak a traceback when <code>DEBUG</code> is on.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0051","card":"q-django-012-detail-view-with-a-path-parameter","sec":"django-s013","q":"What do you need to change when you want a custom 404 page in Django?","a":"<p>Add a <code>404.html</code> template at the templates root and run with <code>DEBUG = False</code>. Django uses it for <code>Http404</code>. You can also set <code>handler404</code> in the root URLconf to a custom view.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0052","card":"q-django-013-5-class-based-views","sec":"django-s014","q":"Why use Django class-based views instead of function-based views?","a":"<p>Generic CBVs implement common patterns (list, detail, create, update, delete) so you set attributes like <code>model</code> and <code>template_name</code> instead of writing the query, 404 handling and form flow. Mixins add behaviour and subclasses override small methods. Trade-off: the inheritance chain can be harder to follow, and for simple custom logic a function view may be clearer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0053","card":"q-django-013-5-class-based-views","sec":"django-s014","q":"Why must you call <code>.as_view()</code> when routing to a class-based view?","a":"<p><code>path()</code> needs a callable that takes a request. <code>as_view()</code> returns such a function. For each request it creates a new instance of the class and dispatches to the method matching the HTTP verb (<code>get</code>, <code>post</code>, ...). Forgetting it passes the class itself and fails.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0054","card":"q-django-013-5-class-based-views","sec":"django-s014","q":"When would you prefer a function-based view in Django?","a":"<p>For one-off actions with no form or generic pattern, such as a button that flips a flag, or when the flow is unusual and the generic hooks would be forced. Function views are explicit and easy to read for small cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0055","card":"q-django-014-templateview-and-mixins","sec":"django-s015","q":"What does <code>TemplateView</code> give you, and how do you pass extra variables to its template?","a":"<p>It renders a template for GET requests with no model needed. Set <code>template_name</code> and give variables with <code>extra_context</code> (a dict) or override <code>get_context_data()</code>. Use <code>get_context_data()</code> for values that must be computed per request, since <code>extra_context</code> is evaluated once at import time.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0056","card":"q-django-014-templateview-and-mixins","sec":"django-s015","q":"In Django, why must <code>LoginRequiredMixin</code> appear before the view class in the base-class list?","a":"<p>Python resolves methods left to right (MRO). <code>LoginRequiredMixin</code> overrides <code>dispatch()</code> to check authentication before the view handles the request. If the view class is first, its <code>dispatch()</code> runs and the check is skipped.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class DashboardView(LoginRequiredMixin, TemplateView):\n    template_name = &#x27;dash.html&#x27;</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"django-f0057","card":"q-django-014-templateview-and-mixins","sec":"django-s015","q":"What replaces <code>@login_required</code> in a class-based view, and how do you set where anonymous users are sent?","a":"<p><code>LoginRequiredMixin</code>. Set <code>login_url</code> on the class (or <code>LOGIN_URL</code> in settings). You can also set <code>redirect_field_name</code>. Anonymous users are redirected to that URL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0058","card":"q-django-014-templateview-and-mixins","sec":"django-s015","q":"What is a mixin in Python and why does Django use them for views?","a":"<p>A mixin is a small class that adds one behaviour and is combined with other classes through multiple inheritance. Django uses them so features like login checking, template rendering and form handling can be combined in any mix to build generic views.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0059","card":"q-django-015-listview-and-detailview","sec":"django-s016","q":"What does a Django <code>ListView</code> do for you with only <code>model = Article</code> set?","a":"<p>It runs <code>Article.objects.all()</code>, renders <code>article_list.html</code> from the app's templates folder (<code>&lt;app&gt;/&lt;model&gt;_list.html</code>), and passes the objects to the template as <code>object_list</code> and <code>article_list</code>. You override <code>template_name</code>, <code>context_object_name</code> or <code>queryset</code> to change these.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0060","card":"q-django-015-listview-and-detailview","sec":"django-s016","q":"What does <code>context_object_name</code> do on a generic view, and what is the default?","a":"<p>It sets the variable name used in the template for the data. For <code>ListView</code> the default is <code>object_list</code> (plus <code>&lt;model&gt;_list</code>), and for <code>DetailView</code> it is <code>object</code> (plus the model name in lower case). A meaningful name makes templates clearer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0061","card":"q-django-015-listview-and-detailview","sec":"django-s016","q":"How does <code>DetailView</code> find the object, and what happens when it does not exist?","a":"<p>It looks up the object using the <code>pk</code> (or <code>slug</code>) captured from the URL and returns a 404 automatically if there is no match. You do not write <code>try/except</code> or <code>Http404</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0062","card":"q-django-015-listview-and-detailview","sec":"django-s016","q":"What is the default template name for a <code>DetailView</code> on model <code>Article</code> in app <code>blog</code>?","a":"<p><code>blog/article_detail.html</code>. The pattern is <code>&lt;app_label&gt;/&lt;model_name_lowercase&gt;_&lt;suffix&gt;.html</code>, so setting <code>template_name</code> is optional when you follow it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0063","card":"q-django-015-listview-and-detailview","sec":"django-s016","q":"Compare function-based and class-based list/detail views in Django.","a":"<p>Function-based: you write the query, the 404 handling and the context dict, and route with <code>views.func</code>. Class-based: the query and 404 are built in, context names come from <code>context_object_name</code>, auth uses <code>LoginRequiredMixin</code>, and you route with <code>views.Class.as_view()</code>. CBVs reduce repetition. Function views give more direct control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0064","card":"q-django-016-custom-queryset-popular-notes","sec":"django-s017","q":"How do you make a Django <code>ListView</code> show only rows with at least one like?","a":"<p>Set the <code>queryset</code> attribute, which replaces the default <code>Model.objects.all()</code>. The filter <code>likes__gte=1</code> means likes greater than or equal to 1. The <code>ListView</code> then uses it for the whole page.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class PopularListView(ListView):\n    model = Article\n    queryset = Article.objects.filter(likes__gte=1)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"django-f0065","card":"q-django-016-custom-queryset-popular-notes","sec":"django-s017","q":"In a Django generic view, when should you use the <code>queryset</code> attribute and when override <code>get_queryset()</code>?","a":"<p><code>queryset</code> is a class attribute evaluated once at import, so it is fine for fixed filters. Use <code>get_queryset()</code> when the filter depends on the request, such as the current user or a query parameter. Request-dependent data must go in the method.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0066","card":"q-django-017-static-files","sec":"django-s018","q":"What are static files in Django and which settings control them?","a":"<p>Static files are CSS, JavaScript and images that do not change per request. <code>STATIC_URL</code> is the URL prefix they are served from. <code>STATICFILES_DIRS</code> lists extra folders to look in, besides each app's <code>static/</code> folder. <code>STATIC_ROOT</code> is the folder <code>collectstatic</code> copies everything into for deployment.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0067","card":"q-django-017-static-files","sec":"django-s018","q":"Why must a template use <code>{% load static %}</code> before <code>{% static 'css/style.css' %}</code>?","a":"<p><code>static</code> is a template tag from a tag library that is not loaded by default. <code>{% load static %}</code> imports it into that template. It must appear in every template that uses the tag, and in a child template it must be repeated because <code>load</code> does not inherit.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0068","card":"q-django-017-static-files","sec":"django-s018","q":"What is the benefit of <code>{% static 'path' %}</code> over hardcoding <code>/static/path</code>?","a":"<p>It builds the URL from <code>STATIC_URL</code>, so you can move static files to a CDN or a different prefix by changing a setting. With a storage like <code>ManifestStaticFilesStorage</code> it also returns hashed file names for cache busting.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0069","card":"q-django-017-static-files","sec":"django-s018","q":"In production with <code>DEBUG = False</code>, why do static files stop loading from <code>runserver</code>, and how do you serve them?","a":"<p>The dev server only serves static files automatically in debug mode. In production you run <code>collectstatic</code> to gather files into <code>STATIC_ROOT</code> and serve them with a web server or CDN, or use something like WhiteNoise.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0070","card":"q-django-017-static-files","sec":"django-s018","q":"What does <code>STATICFILES_DIRS = [BASE_DIR / 'static']</code> do in Django settings?","a":"<p>It adds a project-level <code>static</code> folder to the places Django searches for static files, in addition to each app's <code>static/</code> directory. The <code>BASE_DIR / 'static'</code> path syntax needs <code>BASE_DIR</code> to be a <code>pathlib.Path</code>, which is the default since Django 3.1.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0071","card":"q-django-018-base-template-and-extends","sec":"django-s019","q":"What problem does template inheritance with <code>{% extends %}</code> and <code>{% block %}</code> solve in Django?","a":"<p>It removes duplicated layout. A base template holds the shared HTML (head, navbar, CSS links) with <code>{% block %}</code> placeholders, and child templates extend it and fill only the blocks they need. A layout change is made once.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0072","card":"q-django-018-base-template-and-extends","sec":"django-s019","q":"A child Django template starts with <code>{% extends \"base.html\" %}</code> but Django raises <code>TemplateDoesNotExist</code>. What do you check?","a":"<p>Whether <code>base.html</code> lives in a folder Django searches. Either it is in an app's <code>templates/</code> folder (with <code>APP_DIRS: True</code>) or the folder is listed in <code>TEMPLATES['DIRS']</code>. A project-level base template needs an entry like <code>'DIRS': [BASE_DIR / 'templates']</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0073","card":"q-django-018-base-template-and-extends","sec":"django-s019","q":"Where must <code>{% extends %}</code> appear in a child template, and what happens to content outside blocks?","a":"<p>It must be the first template tag in the file. In a child template, content outside any <code>{% block %}</code> is ignored, because the parent's layout controls the output.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0074","card":"q-django-018-base-template-and-extends","sec":"django-s019","q":"What is <code>{{ block.super }}</code> used for?","a":"<p>Inside a child block it outputs the parent's content for the same block, so you can add to it instead of replacing it. For example you add an extra stylesheet to a <code>head</code> block while keeping the base ones.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0075","card":"q-django-019-named-urls","sec":"django-s020","q":"Why use <code>name=</code> in <code>path()</code> and <code>{% url 'name' %}</code> in templates instead of hardcoded links?","a":"<p>The template looks up the URL from the route name, so changing a route pattern or a prefix in one place updates every link. Hardcoded paths break silently when routes move, and a wrong name fails loudly with <code>NoReverseMatch</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0076","card":"q-django-019-named-urls","sec":"django-s020","q":"How do you pass a path parameter when reversing a URL in a template, and what is the Python equivalent?","a":"<p>In a template: <code>{% url 'article.detail' pk=article.id %}</code>. In Python: <code>reverse('article.detail', args=(pk,))</code> or <code>kwargs={'pk': pk}</code> from <code>django.urls</code>. The names must match the converter names in the route.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0077","card":"q-django-019-named-urls","sec":"django-s020","q":"You get <code>NoReverseMatch</code> from <code>{% url %}</code>. What are the common causes?","a":"<p>The route name is misspelled or not defined, the arguments do not match the converters (wrong names or types), or the app uses a namespace and you did not write <code>'app_name:route'</code>. Check the URLconf and the arguments you pass.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0078","card":"q-django-019-named-urls","sec":"django-s020","q":"What does <code>app_name</code> in an app's <code>urls.py</code> enable?","a":"<p>It sets a URL namespace, so you reverse as <code>{% url 'blog:detail' pk=1 %}</code>. This avoids clashes when two apps use the same route name such as <code>detail</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0079","card":"q-django-020-styled-list-and-detail-templates","sec":"django-s021","q":"What does a template filter such as <code>{{ text|truncatechars:10 }}</code> do, and how are filters written?","a":"<p>A filter transforms a value, applied with <code>|</code>, and may take an argument after a colon. <code>truncatechars:10</code> shortens the string to 10 characters, with the last being an ellipsis. Filters can be chained, e.g. <code>{{ title|lower|truncatechars:20 }}</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0080","card":"q-django-020-styled-list-and-detail-templates","sec":"django-s021","q":"Where should presentation like Bootstrap grid classes live in a Django project, in the view or in the template?","a":"<p>In the template. Views prepare data, and templates decide layout. Shared parts such as the CSS link belong in the base template so every page inherits them.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0081","card":"q-django-021-include-partial-templates","sec":"django-s022","q":"What is the difference between <code>{% extends %}</code> and <code>{% include %}</code> in Django templates?","a":"<p><code>extends</code> is inheritance: a child fills blocks in a parent layout. <code>include</code> is composition: it inserts another template's rendered output at that place. Use <code>extends</code> for page layout and <code>include</code> for reusable fragments such as a card, a list item or a footer.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0082","card":"q-django-021-include-partial-templates","sec":"django-s022","q":"How does an included Django template get its variables?","a":"<p>By default it uses the current context. You can add values with <code>{% include \"x.html\" with title=a.title %}</code> and add <code>only</code> to pass nothing else, which makes the fragment independent of the surrounding context.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0083","card":"q-django-022-7-forms-csrf-and-validation","sec":"django-s023","q":"What does Django's <code>CreateView</code> do, and which attributes do you set at minimum?","a":"<p>It shows a form on GET, and on valid POST saves a new object and redirects. Set <code>model</code> and either <code>fields</code> or <code>form_class</code>, plus <code>success_url</code> (or define <code>get_absolute_url</code> on the model). The default template is <code>&lt;app&gt;/&lt;model&gt;_form.html</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0084","card":"q-django-022-7-forms-csrf-and-validation","sec":"django-s023","q":"Why does Django skip a field like <code>created</code> in <code>fields = ['title', 'text']</code> of a <code>CreateView</code>?","a":"<p>Fields with <code>auto_now_add=True</code> or <code>auto_now=True</code> are not editable, so they cannot be form fields, and the model sets them. Listing <code>fields</code> explicitly also prevents mass assignment of columns the user should not control.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0085","card":"q-django-022-7-forms-csrf-and-validation","sec":"django-s023","q":"Why is <code>fields = '__all__'</code> on a Django <code>ModelForm</code> or <code>CreateView</code> discouraged?","a":"<p>It exposes every model field to the form, including ones added later, such as <code>is_admin</code> or <code>owner</code>. A user could post values for fields they should never set. List the fields you want explicitly.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0086","card":"q-django-022-7-forms-csrf-and-validation","sec":"django-s023","q":"What does <code>{{ form }}</code> render in a Django template, and what are alternatives?","a":"<p>It renders every field with its label and errors, choosing the HTML input from the field type, such as <code>&lt;input&gt;</code> for <code>CharField</code> and <code>&lt;textarea&gt;</code> for <code>TextField</code>. Alternatives are <code>{{ form.as_p }}</code>, <code>{{ form.as_table }}</code> or rendering fields one by one for full control over markup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0087","card":"q-django-022-7-forms-csrf-and-validation","sec":"django-s023","q":"What does <code>success_url</code> do in a Django edit view, and what is the alternative to hardcoding it?","a":"<p>It is where the user is redirected after a successful POST. Instead of a hardcoded string, use <code>reverse_lazy('name')</code> or define <code>get_absolute_url()</code> on the model. This keeps the redirect working if URLs change.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0088","card":"q-django-023-csrf","sec":"django-s024","q":"What is a CSRF attack, and how does Django prevent it?","a":"<p>Cross-Site Request Forgery makes a victim's browser send a state-changing request to a site where they are logged in, using their cookies. Django sends a secret token with each form and checks it on unsafe requests (POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE). A third-party site cannot read the token, so its forged request is rejected.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0089","card":"q-django-023-csrf","sec":"django-s024","q":"A Django POST form returns 403 Forbidden. What is the first thing to check?","a":"<p>Whether the form contains <code>{% csrf_token %}</code>. Without it the <code>CsrfViewMiddleware</code> rejects the request. Also check that <code>CsrfViewMiddleware</code> is enabled, that cookies are accepted, and that a trusted origin is set for HTTPS proxy cases.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0090","card":"q-django-023-csrf","sec":"django-s024","q":"Where must <code>{% csrf_token %}</code> go, and is it needed for a GET form?","a":"<p>Inside every <code>&lt;form method=\"POST\"&gt;</code> that posts to your own site. A GET form does not need it, because GET must be safe and should not change data. Never put the token in a form that posts to an external site, as it would leak.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0091","card":"q-django-023-csrf","sec":"django-s024","q":"How does Django verify the CSRF token in the request flow?","a":"<p>Django sets a CSRF cookie and puts a matching token in the form (or a header for AJAX). On an unsafe request, <code>CsrfViewMiddleware</code> compares the submitted token with the cookie secret. A mismatch or a missing token gives a 403.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0092","card":"q-django-023-csrf","sec":"django-s024","q":"When is <code>@csrf_exempt</code> acceptable in Django?","a":"<p>Rarely. It is used for endpoints called by non-browser clients that do not use cookies, such as webhooks, and those should be protected another way, for example with a signature or token. Do not use it on pages that rely on session cookies.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0093","card":"q-django-024-modelform-and-validation","sec":"django-s025","q":"What is a Django <code>ModelForm</code>, and why use it instead of a plain <code>Form</code>?","a":"<p>A <code>ModelForm</code> builds form fields and validation from a model and can <code>save()</code> to it, so you do not repeat field definitions. A plain <code>Form</code> is for input not tied to a model, such as a contact or search form.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0094","card":"q-django-024-modelform-and-validation","sec":"django-s025","q":"How do you add validation for a single field in a Django form?","a":"<p>Define <code>clean_&lt;fieldname&gt;()</code>. Read the value from <code>self.cleaned_data</code>, raise <code>forms.ValidationError('message')</code> to reject, and always return the value. Django attaches the error to that field and shows it with the form.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>from django import forms\n\nclass ArticleForm(forms.ModelForm):\n    def clean_title(self):\n        title = self.cleaned_data[&#x27;title&#x27;]\n        ...\n        return title</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"django-f0095","card":"q-django-024-modelform-and-validation","sec":"django-s025","q":"Why must a <code>clean_&lt;field&gt;</code> method return the cleaned value?","a":"<p>Django replaces <code>cleaned_data['field']</code> with the returned value. If you forget to return, the field becomes <code>None</code> and the saved data is wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0096","card":"q-django-024-modelform-and-validation","sec":"django-s025","q":"What is the difference between <code>clean_&lt;field&gt;()</code> and <code>clean()</code> in a Django form?","a":"<p><code>clean_&lt;field&gt;()</code> validates one field. <code>clean()</code> runs after all field cleaning and validates rules across fields, such as \"end date must be after start date\". Errors raised in <code>clean()</code> are non-field errors unless you attach them with <code>add_error</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0097","card":"q-django-024-modelform-and-validation","sec":"django-s025","q":"In a <code>ModelForm</code>'s inner <code>Meta</code> class, what do <code>labels</code> and <code>widgets</code> do?","a":"<p><code>labels</code> overrides the text of a field's label. <code>widgets</code> sets the HTML input type and attributes per field, such as <code>forms.Textarea(attrs={'class': 'form-control'})</code>. They let you style the form from Python without changing the template.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0098","card":"q-django-024-modelform-and-validation","sec":"django-s025","q":"What does it mean for a Django form to be \"valid\", and what does <code>is_valid()</code> populate?","a":"<p><code>is_valid()</code> runs field and form validation. On success it fills <code>cleaned_data</code> with validated, type-converted values. On failure it fills <code>form.errors</code>, and you should re-render the form with those errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0099","card":"q-django-024-modelform-and-validation","sec":"django-s025","q":"How do you show only the error for one field in a Django template?","a":"<p>Use <code>form.errors.fieldname</code>, for example <code>{{ form.errors.title.as_text }}</code>, or <code>{{ form.title.errors }}</code>. Wrap it in <code>{% if form.errors %}</code> so the box shows only when there are errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0100","card":"q-django-024-modelform-and-validation","sec":"django-s025","q":"Why validate on the server even if you add HTML <code>required</code> or JavaScript checks?","a":"<p>Client-side checks are only a convenience. A user can bypass the browser with a direct request. The server must be the authority.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0101","card":"q-django-025-codespaces-csrf-fix","sec":"django-s026","q":"What does the <code>CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS</code> setting do, and why might a cloud dev environment need it?","a":"<p>It lists origins whose requests Django accepts for unsafe methods when the request's origin differs from the host Django sees, such as behind a proxy or forwarded HTTPS URL. In Django 4.0+ each entry must include the scheme, for example <code>https://example.com</code>. Earlier versions took host names only.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0102","card":"q-django-025-codespaces-csrf-fix","sec":"django-s026","q":"A Django form fails with \"CSRF verification failed\" behind a reverse proxy even though <code>{% csrf_token %}</code> is present. What do you check?","a":"<p>Whether the Origin header matches a host in <code>ALLOWED_HOSTS</code> or <code>CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS</code>, whether the proxy forwards the host and scheme (<code>SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER</code>, <code>USE_X_FORWARDED_HOST</code>), and whether cookies are being set for the right domain.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0103","card":"q-django-026-updateview","sec":"django-s027","q":"How does Django's <code>UpdateView</code> differ from <code>CreateView</code>?","a":"<p>Both use a model form and <code>form_valid</code> to save. <code>UpdateView</code> loads an existing object by <code>pk</code> from the URL, pre-fills the form with its values, and updates the row. <code>CreateView</code> starts with an empty form and inserts a new row. They share the default template <code>&lt;model&gt;_form.html</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0104","card":"q-django-026-updateview","sec":"django-s027","q":"Why remove the hardcoded <code>action</code> attribute from a form that is reused for create and edit in Django?","a":"<p>With no <code>action</code>, the browser posts to the current URL, so the same template works for both the create URL and the edit URL. A hardcoded <code>action</code> would send edits to the create URL.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0105","card":"q-django-026-updateview","sec":"django-s027","q":"Should the <code>UpdateView</code> URL contain the primary key, and how do you protect other users' records?","a":"<p>Yes, as <code>&lt;int:pk&gt;/edit</code>. But the URL alone gives no ownership check, so restrict the queryset (for example <code>get_queryset</code> filtering by <code>request.user</code>) so another user's pk returns 404, and require login.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0106","card":"q-django-027-deleteview","sec":"django-s028","q":"How does Django's <code>DeleteView</code> work, and why does it need a confirmation template?","a":"<p>A GET renders a confirmation page. A POST deletes the object and redirects to <code>success_url</code>. The two-step flow stops accidental deletion through a link or a crawler, because GET never deletes. The default template is <code>&lt;model&gt;_confirm_delete.html</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0107","card":"q-django-027-deleteview","sec":"django-s028","q":"Why must a delete confirmation form use POST and include <code>{% csrf_token %}</code>?","a":"<p>Deleting changes state, and state changes must not happen on GET, since prefetchers or a malicious image tag could trigger them. POST with a CSRF token ensures the request came from your own page.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0108","card":"q-django-027-deleteview","sec":"django-s028","q":"What happens to related rows when you delete a model instance that others reference by <code>ForeignKey</code>?","a":"<p>It depends on the <code>on_delete</code> option of the relation. <code>CASCADE</code> deletes dependents, <code>PROTECT</code> blocks the delete with <code>ProtectedError</code>, <code>SET_NULL</code> sets the column to null (needs <code>null=True</code>), and <code>SET_DEFAULT</code> uses a default.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0109","card":"q-django-027-deleteview","sec":"django-s028","q":"In Django 4.0+, what is the form-based change to <code>DeleteView</code>?","a":"<p>Since Django 4.0 <code>DeleteView</code> uses <code>FormMixin</code>, so it can take a <code>form_class</code> and a <code>form_valid()</code> hook for the deletion. Before 4.0, you override <code>delete()</code> or <code>post()</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0110","card":"q-django-028-crud-map","sec":"django-s029","q":"Which Django generic class-based views implement Create, Read, Update and Delete, and what default templates do they use?","a":"<p>Create: <code>CreateView</code> with <code>&lt;model&gt;_form.html</code>. Read list: <code>ListView</code> with <code>&lt;model&gt;_list.html</code>. Read one: <code>DetailView</code> with <code>&lt;model&gt;_detail.html</code>. Update: <code>UpdateView</code> with <code>&lt;model&gt;_form.html</code>. Delete: <code>DeleteView</code> with <code>&lt;model&gt;_confirm_delete.html</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0111","card":"q-django-028-crud-map","sec":"django-s029","q":"Design the URL layout for CRUD on a model in Django, and why use <code>&lt;int:pk&gt;</code>?","a":"<p>Typical routes: list <code>items</code>, create <code>items/new</code>, detail <code>items/&lt;int:pk&gt;</code>, update <code>items/&lt;int:pk&gt;/edit</code>, delete <code>items/&lt;int:pk&gt;/delete</code>. <code>&lt;int:pk&gt;</code> captures the primary key as an integer and passes it to the view. Put fixed paths like <code>new</code> before parameterised paths when patterns could overlap.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0112","card":"q-django-028-crud-map","sec":"django-s029","q":"Why can <code>CreateView</code> and <code>UpdateView</code> share one template in Django?","a":"<p>Both use a model form and default to <code>&lt;model&gt;_form.html</code>, and the form's fields are the same. With no hardcoded form <code>action</code>, the same markup posts correctly to whichever URL rendered it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0113","card":"q-django-029-like-button-function-based-view","sec":"django-s030","q":"Why should a \"like\" action be a POST form and not a link in Django?","a":"<p>GET must be safe and must not change data, because links get prefetched, crawled, cached and can be triggered cross-site. POST with <code>{% csrf_token %}</code> is the correct verb for a state change, and Django checks the token.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0114","card":"q-django-029-like-button-function-based-view","sec":"django-s030","q":"What does this view do, and what is the pitfall in how it changes data?","a":"<p>It increments a counter on POST and redirects to the detail page, and non-POST requests get 404. The pitfall is a read-modify-write race: concurrent requests can lose increments. Use <code>F('likes') + 1</code> in <code>update()</code>. Also add <code>@login_required</code> and consider one like per user.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>def add_like(request, pk):\n    if request.method == &#x27;POST&#x27;:\n        item = get_object_or_404(Item, pk=pk)\n        item.likes += 1\n        item.save()\n        return HttpResponseRedirect(reverse(&#x27;item.detail&#x27;, args=(pk,)))\n    raise Http404</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"django-f0115","card":"q-django-029-like-button-function-based-view","sec":"django-s030","q":"Why does a Django view redirect after a successful POST instead of rendering a page?","a":"<p>This is the Post/Redirect/Get pattern. Redirecting prevents a duplicate submission when the user refreshes the page, because the browser reloads the GET page and not the POST.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0116","card":"q-django-029-like-button-function-based-view","sec":"django-s030","q":"What do <code>get_object_or_404</code> and <code>reverse</code> do in Django?","a":"<p><code>get_object_or_404(Model, pk=pk)</code> returns the object or raises <code>Http404</code>. <code>reverse('name', args=(pk,))</code> builds a URL string from a route name in Python code, the counterpart of <code>{% url %}</code> in templates.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0117","card":"q-django-029-like-button-function-based-view","sec":"django-s030","q":"Instead of writing <code>if request.method == 'POST'</code> and raising <code>Http404</code>, what decorator restricts a Django view to certain methods?","a":"<p><code>@require_POST</code> (or <code>@require_http_methods(['POST'])</code>) from <code>django.views.decorators.http</code>. It returns <code>405 Method Not Allowed</code> for other verbs, which is the more accurate status than 404.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0118","card":"q-django-030-foreignkey-to-user","sec":"django-s031","q":"What is a Django <code>ForeignKey</code>, and what do <code>on_delete</code> and <code>related_name</code> control?","a":"<p>A <code>ForeignKey</code> creates a many-to-one relation: many rows point to one user, stored as a <code>user_id</code> column. <code>on_delete</code> says what happens to these rows when the user is deleted (<code>CASCADE</code> deletes them). <code>related_name</code> names the reverse accessor, so <code>user.notes.all()</code> returns that user's rows. Without it the default is <code>&lt;model&gt;_set</code>.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>user = models.ForeignKey(User, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=&#x27;notes&#x27;)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"django-f0119","card":"q-django-030-foreignkey-to-user","sec":"django-s031","q":"Why should a Django model use <code>settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL</code> or <code>get_user_model()</code> instead of importing <code>User</code> directly?","a":"<p>Projects may swap in a custom user model. The setting and helper resolve whichever model is active, so reusable apps and your own code keep working. Starting a project with a custom user model is recommended because changing it later is hard.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0120","card":"q-django-030-foreignkey-to-user","sec":"django-s031","q":"You add a non-null <code>ForeignKey</code> to a table that already has rows. What does Django ask on <code>makemigrations</code>, and what are the options?","a":"<p>It needs a value for existing rows. You can provide a one-off default (such as an existing user's pk), or add the field as <code>null=True</code> first, backfill, then make it required in a later migration. A one-off default works only if that referenced row exists.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0121","card":"q-django-030-foreignkey-to-user","sec":"django-s031","q":"What does <code>on_delete=models.PROTECT</code> do compared with <code>CASCADE</code>?","a":"<p><code>PROTECT</code> raises <code>ProtectedError</code> when you try to delete a referenced row, so data is kept until references are removed. <code>CASCADE</code> silently deletes the referencing rows. Use <code>PROTECT</code> for data you do not want to lose by accident.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0122","card":"q-django-030-foreignkey-to-user","sec":"django-s031","q":"What is the performance issue when a template loops over objects and shows <code>obj.user.username</code> for each, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>Each access runs a separate query (N+1). Use <code>select_related('user')</code> on the queryset to fetch rows and users in one JOIN. For reverse or many-to-many relations use <code>prefetch_related</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0123","card":"q-django-031-filter-by-logged-in-user-get-queryset","sec":"django-s032","q":"In Django's <code>ListView</code>, how do you show only the current user's records?","a":"<p>Override <code>get_queryset()</code> and filter by <code>self.request.user</code>, for example <code>return self.request.user.notes.all()</code> using a related name, or <code>Model.objects.filter(user=self.request.user)</code>. Combine it with <code>LoginRequiredMixin</code>, because anonymous users have no data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0124","card":"q-django-031-filter-by-logged-in-user-get-queryset","sec":"django-s032","q":"How would you find which method of a Django generic view to override?","a":"<p>Read the class hierarchy, for example in the Classy Class-Based Views reference or the source. For <code>ListView</code>, <code>get()</code> calls <code>get_queryset()</code> and then <code>get_context_data()</code>, so <code>get_queryset()</code> is the hook for the query.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0125","card":"q-django-031-filter-by-logged-in-user-get-queryset","sec":"django-s032","q":"What is <code>self.request.user</code> when nobody is logged in?","a":"<p>An <code>AnonymousUser</code> instance with <code>is_authenticated</code> False, so filtering a queryset by it fails. That is why per-user views need <code>LoginRequiredMixin</code> or an <code>is_authenticated</code> check.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0126","card":"q-django-031-filter-by-logged-in-user-get-queryset","sec":"django-s032","q":"Why filter in <code>get_queryset()</code> rather than in the template with <code>{% if note.user == user %}</code>?","a":"<p>Filtering in the query keeps other users' data from being loaded at all, so it is both a security and a performance gain. Hiding rows in the template still fetches them and is easy to get wrong.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0127","card":"q-django-032-assign-the-user-on-create-form-valid","sec":"django-s033","q":"Why does saving a <code>CreateView</code> fail after you add a non-null <code>user</code> ForeignKey, and how do you fix it?","a":"<p>The form does not include <code>user</code>, so the object is saved with no owner and the database rejects it. Override <code>form_valid()</code>, build the object with <code>form.save(commit=False)</code>, set <code>obj.user = self.request.user</code>, save it, and redirect.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0128","card":"q-django-032-assign-the-user-on-create-form-valid","sec":"django-s033","q":"What does <code>form.save(commit=False)</code> do, and when do you use it?","a":"<p>It returns the model instance built from the form without writing it to the database. Use it when you must set extra fields (like the owner) before saving. If the form has many-to-many fields, call <code>form.save_m2m()</code> afterwards.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0129","card":"q-django-032-assign-the-user-on-create-form-valid","sec":"django-s033","q":"In a Django form-handling view, what is the order of events when a user submits a form?","a":"<p>On POST, Django builds the form with <code>request.POST</code>, calls <code>is_valid()</code>, which runs the field validators, <code>clean_&lt;field&gt;()</code> methods and <code>clean()</code>. If valid, <code>form_valid(form)</code> is called. Otherwise <code>form_invalid(form)</code> re-renders the page with errors.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0130","card":"q-django-032-assign-the-user-on-create-form-valid","sec":"django-s033","q":"Why take the owner from <code>request.user</code> and not from a hidden form field?","a":"<p>Hidden fields are user-controlled, so someone could post another user's id. The server-side <code>request.user</code> comes from the authenticated session and cannot be forged.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0131","card":"q-django-032-assign-the-user-on-create-form-valid","sec":"django-s033","q":"Instead of building the redirect by hand, what can <code>form_valid()</code> call to keep the default behaviour?","a":"<p><code>super().form_valid(form)</code>, which saves and redirects to <code>get_success_url()</code>. To change the object first you can set the attribute on <code>form.instance</code> and then call <code>super()</code>, e.g. <code>form.instance.user = self.request.user</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0132","card":"q-django-033-public-private-toggle","sec":"django-s034","q":"How would you let a user make a record public or private in Django?","a":"<p>Add <code>is_public = models.BooleanField(default=False)</code>, run migrations, and add a POST-only view that flips the flag on an object the user owns, with <code>{% csrf_token %}</code> in the form. The default of <code>False</code> keeps new records private.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0133","card":"q-django-033-public-private-toggle","sec":"django-s034","q":"Why is <code>default=False</code> the right default for a visibility flag?","a":"<p>It is secure by default: data stays private until the owner chooses to share. A mistake then leaks nothing.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0134","card":"q-django-033-public-private-toggle","sec":"django-s034","q":"What naming convention is used for boolean model fields, and why?","a":"<p>Name them like yes/no questions, such as <code>is_public</code> or <code>has_paid</code>. Code like <code>if note.is_public</code> then reads naturally.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0135","card":"q-django-033-public-private-toggle","sec":"django-s034","q":"What authorization check is missing from a toggle view that only calls <code>get_object_or_404(Model, pk=pk)</code>?","a":"<p>It does not verify that the object belongs to <code>request.user</code>, so any logged-in user can change any record by guessing the pk. Fetch with the owner filter, such as <code>get_object_or_404(Model, pk=pk, user=request.user)</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0136","card":"q-django-034-10-login-logout-signup-public-share","sec":"django-s035","q":"Which built-in Django views handle login and logout, and how do you use them?","a":"<p><code>django.contrib.auth.views.LoginView</code> and <code>LogoutView</code>. Route them with <code>.as_view()</code> and supply a login template, either through <code>template_name</code> or the default <code>registration/login.html</code>. The login template receives a <code>form</code> (an <code>AuthenticationForm</code>).</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0137","card":"q-django-034-10-login-logout-signup-public-share","sec":"django-s035","q":"What does <code>LOGIN_REDIRECT_URL</code> control, and what is its default?","a":"<p>It is where <code>LoginView</code> sends the user after login when no <code>next</code> parameter is present. The default is <code>/accounts/profile/</code>, which usually does not exist and gives a 404, so set it.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0138","card":"q-django-034-10-login-logout-signup-public-share","sec":"django-s035","q":"What is the difference between <code>LOGIN_URL</code> and <code>LOGIN_REDIRECT_URL</code>?","a":"<p><code>LOGIN_URL</code> is where anonymous users are sent when they hit a protected page. <code>LOGIN_REDIRECT_URL</code> is where logged-in users go after a successful login. <code>LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URL</code> is the same for logout.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0139","card":"q-django-034-10-login-logout-signup-public-share","sec":"django-s035","q":"Why is <code>{{ form.as_p }}</code> used in a login template, and what does it render?","a":"<p>It renders each form field with its label wrapped in a <code>&lt;p&gt;</code> element, a quick default layout. Use manual field rendering when you need custom markup.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0140","card":"q-django-035-auth-aware-navbar-final-base-html","sec":"django-s036","q":"How can a Django template show different buttons for logged-in and anonymous users?","a":"<p>The <code>auth</code> context processor puts <code>user</code> in every template rendered with a <code>RequestContext</code> (that is, <code>render()</code>). Use <code>{% if user.is_authenticated %}...{% else %}...{% endif %}</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0141","card":"q-django-035-auth-aware-navbar-final-base-html","sec":"django-s036","q":"In Django 5.0 and later, how must a logout button be written and why?","a":"<p>As a POST form with <code>{% csrf_token %}</code>. Since Django 5.0 <code>LogoutView</code> no longer accepts GET (deprecated in 4.1). A GET link could log a user out through a prefetch or a cross-site image tag (logout CSRF). In Django 4.x and earlier, a plain link worked.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0142","card":"q-django-035-auth-aware-navbar-final-base-html","sec":"django-s036","q":"Why does the <code>user</code> variable work in every template without being passed from the view?","a":"<p><code>django.contrib.auth.context_processors.auth</code> is enabled in <code>TEMPLATES['OPTIONS']['context_processors']</code>. It adds <code>user</code> and <code>perms</code> to the context of every template rendered with a request.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0143","card":"q-django-035-auth-aware-navbar-final-base-html","sec":"django-s036","q":"Why is hiding a button in the template not enough to secure a feature?","a":"<p>The template only affects what the user sees. The URL can still be requested directly, so the view must enforce authentication and permission itself, for example with <code>LoginRequiredMixin</code>.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0144","card":"q-django-036-signup","sec":"django-s037","q":"How would you build a signup page in Django using built-in parts?","a":"<p>Use a <code>CreateView</code> with <code>form_class = UserCreationForm</code>, a template that renders the form with <code>{% csrf_token %}</code>, and a <code>success_url</code> that sends users to login. <code>UserCreationForm</code> validates the two matching passwords, runs the password validators and stores a hashed password.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0145","card":"q-django-036-signup","sec":"django-s037","q":"What does Django's <code>UserCreationForm</code> give you, and what does it not cover?","a":"<p>It handles username, two password fields, password validation and hashing. It does not collect an email, send verification mail, or protect against bots. For those you add fields, a custom form subclass or a package such as django-allauth.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0146","card":"q-django-036-signup","sec":"django-s037","q":"Why should you never create users with <code>User.objects.create(username=..., password='plain')</code>?","a":"<p>It stores the password unhashed. Use <code>User.objects.create_user(...)</code> or <code>set_password()</code>, which hash the password.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0147","card":"q-django-036-signup","sec":"django-s037","q":"When is it appropriate to use a custom form class on a <code>CreateView</code> for a <code>User</code> and not <code>fields</code>?","a":"<p>When you need password confirmation, extra validation or hashing. A plain <code>fields</code> form on <code>User</code> would store the raw password text, so it is wrong for registration.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0148","card":"q-django-037-public-share-link","sec":"django-s038","q":"How would you let anyone view a note by link, but only if the owner made it public?","a":"<p>Restrict the <code>DetailView</code> queryset to public rows. A private item's pk then does not match, so Django returns 404 and does not reveal that it exists. No login is required because access is decided by the data flag.</p>","hand":true,"qc":"<div class=\"code-tabs flip-code\"><div class=\"tabbar\"><div class=\"tablist\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"tab\" aria-selected=\"true\">Python</button></div><button type=\"button\" class=\"code-copy\" title=\"Copy this code\" aria-label=\"Copy this code\">Copy</button></div><pre><code>class PublicDetailView(DetailView):\n    model = Article\n    queryset = Article.objects.filter(is_public=True)</code></pre></div>"},{"id":"django-f0149","card":"q-django-037-public-share-link","sec":"django-s038","q":"Why return 404 for a private item on a public share URL and not 403?","a":"<p>403 confirms the resource exists. 404 hides it, which leaks nothing about private data.</p>","hand":true},{"id":"django-f0150","card":"q-django-037-public-share-link","sec":"django-s038","q":"What is the risk of sequential integer ids in public share URLs, and what is an alternative?","a":"<p>Ids can be guessed and enumerated, so visitors can probe for public items. Public items are meant to be visible, but a share link should be unguessable if you want it to work like a secret link. Use a <code>UUIDField</code> or a random slug token for the public route.</p>","hand":true}];
